\section{Introduction}
Deep learning has achieved remarkable success across numerous domains, including computer vision, natural language processing, and speech recognition. However, these models often struggle with overfitting, particularly when training data is scarce. To mitigate this issue, data augmentation has become a standard practice in deep learning. This technique involves creating new training samples by applying transformations to existing ones, thereby expanding the dataset and improving model generalization.

While various augmentation strategies exist, most rely on domain-specific knowledge. For instance, in image processing, common techniques include rotation, flipping, and cropping. In contrast, this paper proposes a general augmentation method that does not require such domain-specific insights. Our approach is based on the observation that data typically resides on a low-dimensional manifold. By approximating this manifold with a low-dimensional linear subspace, we can generate new samples by projecting data onto this subspace and sampling from the resulting distribution.

We evaluate the effectiveness of this method on both synthetic and real-world datasets. Our results indicate that the proposed approach yields significant performance improvements. Additionally, we demonstrate that this method can be combined with other augmentation techniques to further enhance model performance.

