\section*{1. Introduction}

Ensemble learning has become a cornerstone of machine learning, with its efficacy in boosting predictive performance widely acknowledged. The primary objective of ensemble learning is to aggregate the predictions of multiple models to achieve superior results compared to any single constituent model. This approach is particularly effective when the individual models exhibit diversity, meaning they make distinct errors. Consequently, the central challenge in ensemble learning is to construct an ensemble that maximizes the diversity among its members.

The concept of diversity is fundamental to ensemble learning, as it is widely accepted that greater diversity leads to improved performance. However, the precise definition of diversity remains ambiguous, and no consensus exists on how to measure it. This lack of clarity has led to the development of numerous diversity metrics, each with its own theoretical and empirical characteristics. This paper aims to clarify the relationship between model diversity and ensemble accuracy, thereby offering guidance for practitioners and researchers seeking to optimize ensemble performance.

