Domain adaptation (DA) aims to transfer knowledge from a source domain to a target domain, a task that is particularly critical in real-world scenarios where labeled data is often scarce. A primary challenge in DA is the domain shift, which arises from differences in data distributions between the source and target domains. To address this, numerous methods have been developed to align these distributions, with adversarial training being a prominent strategy. This technique employs a domain discriminator to learn domain-invariant features, thereby reducing the domain shift. However, adversarial training often suffers from instability and may fail to capture the underlying structure of the data.

In this paper, we introduce a new framework for universal domain adaptation that explicitly incorporates data structure. We define a structure-aware loss function to align the structural information of both domains. This approach is model-agnostic, meaning it can be integrated with any pre-trained model. Our method is straightforward to implement and achieves state-of-the-art results on various benchmark datasets. Additionally, we demonstrate that applying our method to pre-trained models yields significant performance improvements on target domains.

