\section{Introduction}
Class imbalance is a prevalent challenge in supervised learning, where the distribution of classes is uneven. This issue is particularly common in real-world applications, such as medical diagnosis, where the number of positive cases is often significantly lower than negative ones. Consequently, standard supervised learning algorithms tend to favor the majority class, leading to poor performance on the minority class. To address this, various methods have been developed, including resampling techniques like oversampling and undersampling, as well as cost-sensitive learning approaches that assign higher penalties to misclassifications of the minority class.

However, most existing solutions focus on binary classification. In multi-class problems, the imbalance issue becomes more complex because the minority class may overlap with multiple majority classes. This overlap can cause the model to misclassify minority instances as belonging to a majority class. Therefore, it is essential to measure the extent of this overlap to effectively mitigate it.

In this paper, we introduce a new method to measure class overlap using the Bhattacharyya coefficient, a metric that quantifies the similarity between two probability distributions. We show that this coefficient can be used to assess the separability of classes. Additionally, we propose a simple and effective strategy to reduce class overlap by modifying the class weights in the loss function. Our experimental results demonstrate that this approach surpasses current state-of-the-art methods. The source code can be found at https://github.com/abhishek1999/Class_Overlap.

