\section{Introduction}
Machine learning has become a cornerstone of modern data analysis, with decision trees and their ensembles being particularly popular due to their interpretability and resilience. Despite these advantages, they remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks, where adversaries craft subtle input perturbations to deceive the model. This study presents a new attack strategy that leverages the interpretability of decision trees. By examining the decision paths of a trained tree, we pinpoint the most critical features and create adversarial examples by altering them. We validate the attack's effectiveness against both individual and ensemble decision trees. Additionally, we demonstrate that the attack succeeds even when the adversary does not know the target model's structure. Our findings show that this method outperforms current state-of-the-art techniques across multiple datasets. The source code can be accessed at https://github.com/abhishek199901/DecisionTreeAdversarialAttack.

